---
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';
import QA from '../components/QA.astro';
import Figure from '../components/Figure.astro';
import Math from '../components/Math.astro';

const swatches = [
  { token: '--paper', use: 'Page background' },
  { token: '--paper-raised', use: 'Figures, code' },
  { token: '--paper-shade', use: 'Inline code' },
  { token: '--ink', use: 'Body text' },
  { token: '--ink-soft', use: 'Secondary text' },
  { token: '--red', use: 'Accents, mistakes' },
  { token: '--blue', use: 'Links, interview' },
  { token: '--green', use: 'Plain words' },
  { token: '--gold', use: 'Revision' },
  { token: '--highlight', use: 'Technical terms' },
];

const sample = `import torch

x = torch.tensor([1.0, 2.0, 3.0])
w = torch.tensor([0.5, -1.0, 2.0])
z = (w * x).sum() + 0.1   # weighted sum plus bias`;
---

<Base title="Style guide">
  <div class="prose">
    <p class="eyebrow">The design system</p>
    <h1>Style guide</h1>
    <p class="lede">Every building block the notes are made of, and the rules for writing them.</p>

    <h2>Writing rules</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Simple first.</strong> Open every idea with an everyday explanation that needs no background.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Precise second.</strong> Then give the exact technical term, highlighted like
        <span class="term">this</span>, because the everyday version cannot be said in an interview.
      </li>
      <li><strong>Show it.</strong> Every note has at least one diagram of the mechanism, redrawable from memory.</li>
      <li><strong>Say it.</strong> Every note has a model spoken answer and practice questions.</li>
      <li><strong>Source it.</strong> Every note lists the book chapters, papers and lectures it draws on.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Anatomy of a note</h2>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Section</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>In plain words</td><td>The idea with no jargon.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>The picture</td><td>A diagram of how it works.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>How it really works</td><td>The full explanation with proper terms.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Math and code</td><td>Only what is needed, explained line by line.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Say it in the interview</td><td>A polished answer to speak.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Practice questions</td><td>Common questions with answers, hidden until opened.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Common mistakes</td><td>What candidates get wrong.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Sources</td><td>Where to read more.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>One-minute revision</td><td>The whole note on one card.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Colour</h2>
    <p>Each pen colour has one job, so a glance at a page tells you what kind of block you are looking at.</p>
    <div class="swatches">
      {
        swatches.map((s) => (
          <div class="swatch">
            <i style={`background: var(${s.token})`} />
            <span>
              <code>{s.token}</code>
              <br />
              {s.use}
            </span>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>

    <h2>Type</h2>
    <p>
      Body text is <strong>Literata</strong>, a typeface designed for long reading. Labels and diagram text use
      <span style="font-family: var(--hand); font-size: 1.4rem">Caveat, a handwriting face</span>. Code uses
      <code>JetBrains Mono</code>.
    </p>
    <p class="scribble">A handwritten remark, used rarely, for the one thing to remember.</p>

    <h2>Blocks</h2>
    <Callout kind="plain">
      <p>The everyday explanation. Green always means “start here”.</p>
    </Callout>
    <Callout kind="interview">
      <p>A model answer in full sentences, written to be spoken in one to two minutes.</p>
    </Callout>
    <Callout kind="pitfall">
      <p>Something candidates commonly get wrong, and the correct version.</p>
    </Callout>
    <Callout kind="note">
      <p>A side remark: history, a link to another note, or a detail that is good to know.</p>
    </Callout>
    <Callout kind="revision">
      <ul>
        <li>The whole note in five or six bullets.</li>
        <li>Read only these the night before an interview.</li>
      </ul>
    </Callout>

    <h2>Practice questions</h2>
    <QA q="What does a question look like before it is opened?">
      <p>Like this. The answer stays hidden so you can try first.</p>
    </QA>
    <QA q="And printed?">
      <p>Answers are always shown on paper.</p>
    </QA>

    <h2>Diagrams</h2>
    <p>
      Diagrams are drawn as ink on the page: slightly wobbly lines, handwritten labels, and the same wash colours as
      the blocks. They are inline SVG, so they stay sharp at any size and need no image files.
    </p>
    <Figure n={1} caption="Boxes are things, arrows are data flowing, red ink marks what to notice.">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 720 150" role="img" aria-label="Sample diagram: input, model, output">
        <g class="sketch">
          <rect class="stroke fill-gold" x="40" y="40" width="150" height="70" rx="6"></rect>
          <rect class="stroke fill-green" x="285" y="40" width="150" height="70" rx="6"></rect>
          <rect class="stroke fill-blue" x="530" y="40" width="150" height="70" rx="6"></rect>
          <path class="stroke" d="M195 75 H278" marker-end="url(#arrow)"></path>
          <path class="stroke" d="M440 75 H523" marker-end="url(#arrow)"></path>
        </g>
        <text x="115" y="82" text-anchor="middle">input</text>
        <text x="360" y="82" text-anchor="middle">model</text>
        <text x="605" y="82" text-anchor="middle">output</text>
        <text class="small label-red" x="360" y="135" text-anchor="middle">the part being explained</text>
      </svg>
    </Figure>

    <h2>Code and math</h2>
    <Code code={sample} lang="python" theme="vitesse-light" />
    <p>Inline math such as <Math tex="z = w \cdot x + b" /> sits in the sentence. Important equations get their own line:</p>
    <Math block tex="\text{softmax}(z_i) = \frac{e^{z_i}}{\sum_j e^{z_j}}" />

    <hr />
    <p>A section break, for a change of subject inside one note.</p>
  </div>
</Base>
